1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,360
On April 16, 1991, Bernie Madoff
said he bought 123 call options on

2
00:00:06,380 --> 00:00:08,070
Procter & Gamble stock.

3
00:00:08,260 --> 00:00:09,290
One problem.

4
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:12,060
In the entire market, that whole day,

5
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:14,810
only 20 of those options changed hands.

6
00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:17,820
He claimed six times more than existed,

7
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:21,190
and nobody stopped him for 17 more years.

8
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:25,930
By the time it collapsed, the
fake balances said $65 billion.

9
00:00:27,150 --> 00:00:27,860
Here's the thing.

10
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:31,070
That impossible number isn't a fluke.

11
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:33,840
The biggest frauds in
history all hide one.

12
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:36,070
A number that cannot exist.

13
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:37,940
Today, three of them.

14
00:00:37,960 --> 00:00:40,210
A coupon scam from 1920.

15
00:00:40,280 --> 00:00:42,300
A tank farm full of water.

16
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:44,850
And the fund that almost never lost.

17
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:47,380
And by the end, you'll
have a three question test

18
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:49,140
that would have caught all three.

19
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:52,570
Because the next one won't
call itself a Ponzi scheme.

20
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:59,350
Chapter 1 The Coupon That
Couldn't Exist Boston, 1920

21
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:02,770
Banks are paying about 5% a year.

22
00:01:02,940 --> 00:01:09,010
And a former dishwasher named Charles
Ponzi is promising 50% in 45 days.

23
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:10,950
His pitch sounded clever.

24
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:13,310
International reply coupons.

25
00:01:13,540 --> 00:01:15,740
Little slips you could
buy cheap in Europe, where

26
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:18,900
money was weak after the
war, and trade in America for

27
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:20,410
stamps worth more.

28
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:23,210
Buy low there, cash in high here.

29
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:25,440
On paper, that's arbitrage.

30
00:01:25,460 --> 00:01:26,550
It's even legal.

31
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:29,090
And for a while, it looked like it worked.

32
00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:31,020
Early investors got paid.

33
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:32,010
On time.

34
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:33,250
Word spread.

35
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:37,830
By July, money was pouring in at
close to a million dollars a day.

36
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:40,570
Even most of Boston's
police force bought in.

37
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:42,750
Hit like if you'd have bought in too.

38
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:44,350
Most of Boston did.

39
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:46,390
But nobody did the math.

40
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:49,980
Until a financial journalist
named Clarence Barron did.

41
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,960
To cover what Ponzi owed, 160 million
postal coupons would have to be in

42
00:01:54,980 --> 00:01:55,480
circulation.

43
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:57,310
The actual number?

44
00:01:57,460 --> 00:01:59,510
About 27,000.

45
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:01,200
That's not a little short.

46
00:02:01,220 --> 00:02:04,750
That's about one coupon
for every 6,000 he'd need.

47
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:06,930
So where were the profits coming from?

48
00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:08,570
The next investor.

49
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:11,760
Old investors were paid
with new investors' money.

50
00:02:11,780 --> 00:02:13,070
That's the whole trick.

51
00:02:13,220 --> 00:02:15,680
When the papers ran the
numbers, the line outside his

52
00:02:15,700 --> 00:02:17,610
office turned into a run.

53
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:19,490
Six banks went down.

54
00:02:19,940 --> 00:02:22,330
Investors lost about 20 million dollars.

55
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:26,300
In 1920, Ponzi pleaded
guilty to mail fraud.

56
00:02:26,320 --> 00:02:28,610
His name became the word for the scam,

57
00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:32,450
and he died broke with
an estate of 75 dollars.

58
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:36,590
Ponzi's impossible number
was hiding in coupons.

59
00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:40,630
The next one was hiding in
plain sight, inside a tank.

60
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:47,690
Chapter 2 More Oil Than America
New Jersey The early 1960s.

61
00:02:48,440 --> 00:02:52,760
Tino De Angelis runs a vegetable oil
company called Allied Crude out of

62
00:02:52,780 --> 00:02:54,990
a rundown tank farm in Bayonne.

63
00:02:55,080 --> 00:02:57,870
His business ran on a simple kind of loan.

64
00:02:58,340 --> 00:02:59,750
Oil sits in a tank.

65
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:03,010
A warehouse company checks
the tank and writes a receipt.

66
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:05,680
Banks lend money against the receipt.

67
00:03:05,700 --> 00:03:07,530
And the company writing those receipts?

68
00:03:07,820 --> 00:03:09,770
A unit of American Express.

69
00:03:10,220 --> 00:03:12,580
If American Express vouched for the oil,

70
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:13,690
the banks paid.

71
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,130
So, De Angelis made more oil.

72
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:18,730
Not real oil, receipt oil.

73
00:03:18,840 --> 00:03:22,600
Some tanks had hidden compartments,
so inspectors saw oil at the hatch.

74
00:03:22,620 --> 00:03:26,190
Others were mostly water,
because oil floats.

75
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:29,830
Inspectors looked at the
top, saw oil, and signed.

76
00:03:29,880 --> 00:03:31,740
And when they walked to the next tank,

77
00:03:31,760 --> 00:03:34,300
pipes moved the same oil ahead of them.

78
00:03:34,320 --> 00:03:37,670
The same oil, counted again and again.

79
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:39,690
Here's the impossible number.

80
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:44,120
By 1963, Allied claimed more
soybean and cottonseed oil

81
00:03:44,140 --> 00:03:47,170
than existed in the entire United States.

82
00:03:47,280 --> 00:03:50,210
On paper, 900,000 tons.

83
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:53,600
Actually there, about 55,000.

84
00:03:53,620 --> 00:03:56,430
Over 90% of it never existed.

85
00:03:56,760 --> 00:04:00,510
Send this to the friend who trusts
anything that comes with a receipt.

86
00:04:00,940 --> 00:04:03,770
Then he tried to corner
the market in oil futures.

87
00:04:04,040 --> 00:04:06,750
When prices cracked in November 1963,

88
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:10,990
the margin calls came, and somebody
finally looked inside the tanks.

89
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:12,690
51 lenders.

90
00:04:12,900 --> 00:04:16,030
More than $180 million in losses.

91
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:18,660
The American Express
warehouse unit went bankrupt.

92
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:21,290
A Wall Street brokerage went under.

93
00:04:21,440 --> 00:04:24,810
DeAngelis pleaded guilty and
was sentenced to 20 years.

94
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:26,630
He was out in seven.

95
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:27,830
Coupons.

96
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:28,610
Tanks.

97
00:04:29,020 --> 00:04:32,750
Both frauds needed more of something
than the world actually had.

98
00:04:32,840 --> 00:04:36,420
But the biggest one in history
needed something even rarer.

99
00:04:36,440 --> 00:04:38,090
A perfect record.

100
00:04:38,940 --> 00:04:40,010
Chapter 3.

101
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:42,770
The fund that almost never lost.

102
00:04:43,360 --> 00:04:45,410
Bernie Madoff wasn't a nobody.

103
00:04:45,560 --> 00:04:47,020
He'd been chairman of the NASDAQ.

104
00:04:47,040 --> 00:04:51,330
His trading firm was one of the
biggest market makers on Wall Street.

105
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:55,190
And his investment fund had a
pitch that sounded almost boring.

106
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:56,340
No 50%.

107
00:04:56,400 --> 00:04:58,330
Just steady returns.

108
00:04:58,400 --> 00:05:00,030
Around 10% a year.

109
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:02,710
Year after year after year.

110
00:05:02,940 --> 00:05:04,810
That was the genius of it.

111
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:06,490
Ponzi sold greed.

112
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:08,530
Madoff sold safety.

113
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:13,370
One fund reported 10.5%
a year for 17 years.

114
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:17,820
In late 2008, while markets were
crashing, it said it was still up

115
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:18,950
for the year.

116
00:05:19,240 --> 00:05:21,540
Now picture a real investment chart.

117
00:05:21,560 --> 00:05:22,420
It wiggles.

118
00:05:22,440 --> 00:05:23,360
It drops.

119
00:05:23,380 --> 00:05:25,770
It has bad months and bad years.

120
00:05:26,140 --> 00:05:30,610
Madoff's line went up at an
almost perfect 45 degree angle.

121
00:05:30,880 --> 00:05:33,700
A financial analyst named
Harry Markopolos was asked

122
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:35,610
to copy Madoff's strategy.

123
00:05:35,760 --> 00:05:38,240
He later said he knew
within five minutes that

124
00:05:38,260 --> 00:05:39,860
the numbers didn't add up.

125
00:05:39,880 --> 00:05:42,360
It took him four hours to prove it.

126
00:05:42,380 --> 00:05:43,860
His red flags were math.

127
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:48,630
Madoff reported losing months
only about 4% of the time.

128
00:05:48,820 --> 00:05:50,790
Real markets don't move like that.

129
00:05:50,840 --> 00:05:53,170
And the strategy itself was impossible.

130
00:05:53,420 --> 00:05:55,420
To make the trades Madoff claimed,

131
00:05:55,440 --> 00:05:58,560
he would have needed more options on
the Chicago Board Options Exchange

132
00:05:58,580 --> 00:06:00,470
than actually existed.

133
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:02,470
Same pattern as 1991.

134
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:06,330
123 options claimed, 20 traded.

135
00:06:06,620 --> 00:06:09,850
Markopolos took his
math to the SEC in 2000.

136
00:06:10,060 --> 00:06:10,460
And again.

137
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:15,000
In 2005, he sent a memo titled,
The world's largest hedge

138
00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:16,590
fund is a fraud.

139
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:21,690
Over 16 years, regulators looked at
Madoff's firm at least eight times.

140
00:06:21,760 --> 00:06:23,310
They didn't find the fraud.

141
00:06:23,660 --> 00:06:25,770
Then, 2008 happened.

142
00:06:26,140 --> 00:06:29,120
Markets crashed and investors
asked for their money back.

143
00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:31,560
About $7 billion of it.

144
00:06:31,580 --> 00:06:35,700
Madoff's bank account, which had held
over $5 billion earlier that year,

145
00:06:35,720 --> 00:06:38,790
was down to about $234 million.

146
00:06:38,900 --> 00:06:43,570
Subscribe now, because this next
part is where it all comes apart.

147
00:06:43,720 --> 00:06:47,760
On December 10th, he told his sons
he was finished and that the fund was

148
00:06:47,780 --> 00:06:49,030
a Ponzi scheme.

149
00:06:49,260 --> 00:06:50,710
They went to the authorities.

150
00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:53,030
The next morning, he was arrested.

151
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:58,790
Prosecutors put the fraud at $64.8
billion based on the fake statements.

152
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:02,320
Markopolos estimated that
$35 to $55 billion of

153
00:07:02,340 --> 00:07:04,880
that was profit that never existed at all.

154
00:07:04,900 --> 00:07:08,440
Madoff pleaded guilty to
11 federal crimes and was

155
00:07:08,460 --> 00:07:11,030
sentenced to 150 years.

156
00:07:11,220 --> 00:07:13,490
He died in prison in 2021.

157
00:07:14,460 --> 00:07:16,850
So here's the impossible number test.

158
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:17,930
Three questions.

159
00:07:18,160 --> 00:07:19,910
They would have caught all three.

160
00:07:20,440 --> 00:07:21,230
Question one.

161
00:07:21,400 --> 00:07:24,130
Does the math need more
of something than exists?

162
00:07:24,420 --> 00:07:25,450
More coupons.

163
00:07:25,620 --> 00:07:26,630
More oil.

164
00:07:26,720 --> 00:07:27,850
More options.

165
00:07:28,120 --> 00:07:32,170
If a return needs a market bigger
than the market, it's not a return.

166
00:07:32,540 --> 00:07:33,080
Question two.

167
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:35,130
Is it too smooth?

168
00:07:35,320 --> 00:07:36,890
Real money wiggles.

169
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:40,140
If something never has a
bad month, that's not skill.

170
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:41,330
That's a story.

171
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:42,670
Question three.

172
00:07:42,740 --> 00:07:44,810
Can anyone check it besides them?

173
00:07:44,960 --> 00:07:47,350
Ponzi kept his books on index cards.

174
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:50,350
De Angelis's inspectors
had close ties to him.

175
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:53,890
Madoff ran his own brokerage,
so he checked his own homework.

176
00:07:54,360 --> 00:07:55,280
Three frauds.

177
00:07:55,300 --> 00:07:56,670
Three different eras.

178
00:07:56,800 --> 00:07:57,740
One pattern.

179
00:07:57,760 --> 00:07:59,870
A number that can't exist.

180
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,880
So next time someone shows
you a perfect chart, don't

181
00:08:02,900 --> 00:08:04,480
ask how much you could make.

182
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:06,730
Ask what it would take for it to be true.

183
00:08:07,020 --> 00:08:10,000
Subscribe for the next case, and
tell me in the comments which red

184
00:08:10,020 --> 00:08:12,010
flag would have caught you first.

185
00:08:12,160 --> 00:08:15,170
Then, watch the tank that
fooled American Express.

